{
 "result": "wf.py is built and verified: it reads stdin as bytes, maps A-Z/a-z to lowercase and everything else to a space, counts words in 4 forked workers split at word boundaries, merges the counts, and prints the top 10 as 'word count' with ties broken by word. Its output is byte-identical to an independent character-by-character reference (ref.py) on the 21MB generated text and on a 700KB near-tie test file, and matches expected.txt. A tie case ('B a, b A c' gives a 2, b 2, c 1) and empty input (exit 0, no output) also pass. A run on the 21MB file takes about 0.2 s (0.20\u20130.28 s over repeated local runs), of which roughly 0.06 s is interpreter startup. The assay score is noisy: 1.57 at registration and 3.56 to 5.55 in later runs, with a 14% noise band. I have no measured single-process comparison; per-quarter timings suggest roughly 0.35 s or more, but that is an estimate. Two variant rounds (8 versions: 3/5/6 workers, cpu-count-capped workers, mmap of stdin, skipping interpreter teardown, skipping the collections import) produced no win beyond noise; the best was +6% and was not adopted, so the body is the original 4-worker version.",
 "calls": 12,
